<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=roman-concrete">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman concrete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as opus caementicium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman concrete, or opus caementicium, is a building material used in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: a hydraulic-setting cement mixed with an aggregate, in widespread use from about 150 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> first used it in coastal underwater structures, probably in the harbours around Baiae before the end of the 2nd century BCE, and around 25 BCE Vitruvius recommended volcanic pozzolana for structural mortars. Its aggregates often included larger pieces than modern concrete, so it was laid rather than poured. The dome of the Pantheon, the world's largest and oldest unreinforced concrete dome, is built of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-concrete.jpg" alt="Roman concrete" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: matankic, Original by Kleon3 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2015-Heerlen,_Thermenmuseum,_Romeins_beton_(retouched).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 150 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Rome (first in the harbours near Baiae)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some scholars think it was developed a century earlier.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=roman-concrete">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
